Understanding information diffusion in microblogging platforms matters for civic discourse, yet the full system is too extensive to reproduce feature by feature. This thesis asks a narrower, falsifiable question: are the irreducible mechanics of the platform --- an asymmetric follow graph, a reverse-chronological timeline and repost amplification, driven by the users' own activity rhythms --- sufficient to reproduce the underlying dynamics of diffusion? To answer it, a high-performance Discrete-Event Simulation (DES) engine, implemented in Zig, formalizes the platform as a Time-Varying Heterogeneous Graph and couples the Continuous-Time Independent Cascade (CTIC) model with activity-driven user sessions; DES is the natural paradigm because user activity is itself a sequence of discrete, asynchronous events --- session starts and ends, post creations and interactions --- serviced by reverse-chronological timeline queues. The model is calibrated end to end on telemetry extracted from the open Bluesky Firehose --- per-user session, posting and interaction distributions, and a forest-fire-sampled follower topology. Executed on up to one million users, the engine scales near-linearly and reaches a stationary regime. The calibrated core reproduces the qualitative shape of the empirical cascades --- tiny, shallow and broadcast-dominated, with the same lognormal repost distribution --- but only about half the empirical rate of non-trivial cascades, and it cannot reach their heavy tail; the reasons are located rather than residual. Depth is bounded by the subcritical reproduction number that content-agnostic behaviour imposes on every cascade. Width is not explained by the reverse-chronological order, which the simulation shows to be second-order, but by the impression budget and, most strongly, by an inflated post arrival rate: the calibration samples only the active minority of users, so simulated timelines receive far more content than real ones. Together with in-network-only impressions and a uniform conversion rate, these caps specify the mechanism the model lacks --- post-level heterogeneity and out-of-network impressions, i.e. content-aware behaviour and recommendation.

*Keywords*: Information Diffusion, Discrete-Event Simulation (DES), Continuous-Time Independent Cascade (CTIC), Bluesky/AT Protocol, Activity-Driven Networks, Branching Processes, Structural Virality
